    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a> mined fossil water from under the desert through a network of tunnels and shafts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foggara">foggaras</a>, which supported farming and a large population in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>.</li><li>Their largest town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garama">Garama</a>, had about four thousand inhabitants, with another six thousand in villages within a 5 km radius, and up to 50,000 people may have lived in the whole wadi.</li><li>The fall of the ground water level over the centuries of the kingdom may have contributed to its end in the late 7th century AD.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Roman traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-maternus">Julius Maternus</a> journeyed with the king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantes</a> for about four months from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garama">Garama</a> to a land called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agisymba">Agisymba</a>, whose people the king presented as his subjects.</p></aside>
    </section>

